Implemented `ch8_lost_updates.sql`:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION atomic_increment(p_page_id TEXT)
RETURNS BIGINT
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  new_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
  UPDATE page_views
  SET view_count = view_count + 1
  WHERE page_id = p_page_id
  RETURNING view_count INTO new_count;

  RETURN new_count;
END;
$$;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION compare_and_set(
  p_page_id TEXT,
  p_expected_version BIGINT,
  p_new_count BIGINT
) RETURNS BOOLEAN
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  updated_rows INTEGER;
BEGIN
  UPDATE page_views
  SET view_count = p_new_count,
      version = version + 1
  WHERE page_id = p_page_id
    AND version = p_expected_version;

  GET DIAGNOSTICS updated_rows = ROW_COUNT;
  RETURN updated_rows = 1;
END;
$$;
```

The lost-update reproduction ended at `view_count = 1` after two transactions each read 0 and wrote 1. `atomic_increment` performs one atomic update and returns its `RETURNING` value. `compare_and_set` changes the row only at the expected version and reports whether exactly one row was updated.
